# SLIC 2

MATLAB experiments for groupwise whole-brain parcellation using five normalized-cut and SLIC-based methods.

[GPL-3.0 license](LICENSE) · [Technical notes](Readme_plus)

## Quick Start

1. Prepare MATLAB with Parallel Computing Toolbox.
2. Download and extract the preprocessed 40-subject demo data from the [NITRC project](https://www.nitrc.org/frs/?group_id=1030) into `data/`.
3. Review [main_preparation.m](main_preparation.m): it discovers `data/sub*` files, uses cluster counts from 50 to 1000, and constructs random group splits with a hardcoded 40-subject assumption. Adapt that logic before using a different cohort size.
4. Set an appropriate `nWorker` value; the default is 90% of available processors. Then run from the repository directory:

```matlab
main
```

Preparation attempts to download and extract `NIfTI_20140122.zip`. The dependency endpoint and a current runtime environment have not been revalidated. The original notes report about 10 hours on a server with 40 CPUs and 256 GB memory; this is a historical measurement.

## Changes Documented in This Version

- Eigenvectors corresponding to trivial eigenvalues are no longer discarded.
- Iteration tolerance is set to `1e-3`, with a maximum of 100 iterations.
- Shared configuration is stored in `sInfo.mat`.

## Repository Structure

- [main.m](main.m): full experiment.
- [main_preparation.m](main_preparation.m): inputs, parameters, and preparation.
- [main_algorithm.m](main_algorithm.m): parcellation methods.
- [main_evaluation.m](main_evaluation.m): evaluation.
- [main_plot.m](main_plot.m), [main_illustration.m](main_illustration.m), and [main_histogram.m](main_histogram.m): visual outputs.
- [Readme_plus](Readme_plus): original data, preprocessing, software, and hardware notes.

## Citation

Jing Wang and Haixian Wang. *A supervoxel-based method for groupwise whole brain parcellation with resting-state fMRI data.* Frontiers in Human Neuroscience. [doi:10.3389/fnhum.2016.00659](https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2016.00659).

## Related Projects

- [SLIC](https://github.com/yuzhounh/SLIC): related implementation listed in the original documentation.
- [SLIC on NITRC](https://www.nitrc.org/projects/slic/): project and demo data.

